.TH BITS 1
.SH NAME
bits \- how much information each part of a text carries, according to a language model
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bits
[
.B -p
] [
.B -m
.I dir
] [
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Bits
reads a text from
.I file
or its standard input, writes it to a session of the language model served at
.I dir
.RB ( /mnt/lm ,
by
.I lmfs
or
.IR lmsim (4)),
and prints the model's answer.
.PP
With no option it prints a line for each token:
.IP
.I "q0 q1 bits rank"
.LP
.I Q0
and
.I q1
are rune offsets into the text, which serve as
.IR acme (1)
addresses,
.I bits
is the token's surprisal, the negative base-2 logarithm of its probability, to two places,
and
.I rank
is where the token stood among the model's guesses, 1 for the likeliest.
The server joins a token that ends inside a rune with the next, so every line starts and ends on
a rune.
The lines tile the text.
The
.I bits
column sums to the text's size under the model, and 2 to the power of its mean is the
model's perplexity on the text.
.PP
With
.B -p
it prints a line for each paragraph:
.IP
.I "q0 q1 bits alone shared"
.LP
A paragraph is a run of lines with text, and the blank lines after it;
the first takes any blank lines before it, so the paragraphs tile the text.
.I Bits
is the paragraph's cost given the text before it, the sum of its tokens' bits,
.I alone
its cost as a text of its own, and
.I shared
the difference: how much of it the text has already said.
.B -p
sets the session's context to the longest the model takes, its
.B maxctx
in
.BR ctl ,
so that
.I shared
sees repetition from far back.
.PP
The model measures surprise, not value: a cliché costs few bits, and a misspelling and a fresh
idea many.
.SH EXAMPLES
The ten costliest runes of a file:
.IP
.EX
bits file | sort -nr +2 | sed 10q
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B src/cmd/bits.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR segment (1),
.IR lmsim (4),
.B doc/words.md
